            your riding ability, equipment and range.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </header>

      <div className="article-main-grid article-shell">
        <aside className="article-toc">
          <p>IN THIS GUIDE</p>
          <a href="#why-emtb">Why choose an E-MTB?</a>
          <a href="#where-to-ride">Where to ride in Morocco</a>
          <a href="#choose-tour">Choose your tour</a>
          <a href="#battery-range">Battery range and charging</a>
          <a href="#skills-fitness">Skills and fitness</a>
          <a href="#bike-equipment">Bike and equipment</a>
          <a href="#seasons">Weather and seasons</a>
          <a href="#responsible-riding">Responsible riding</a>
          <a href="#plan-trip">Plan your E-MTB trip</a>
          <a href="#checklist">Pre-trip checklist</a>
          <a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a>
        </aside>

        <article className="article-body">
          <p className="article-lead">
            An E-MTB tour is more than a conventional mountain bike trip with a
            motor added. Electric assistance changes how riders approach
            climbing, manage their energy and plan a day on the trail. In
            Morocco, the best experience comes from matching the bike and
            battery setup to the landscape, the riding group and the route
            ahead.
          </p>

          <section id="why-emtb">
            <h2>Why choose an E-MTB tour in Morocco?</h2>
            <p>
              Morocco offers a remarkable variety of cycling landscapes.
              Around the Atlas Mountains, routes can cross open hillsides,
              rocky tracks, terraced valleys and traditional villages. An
              electric mountain bike can help riders manage sustained climbs
              and conserve energy for the rest of the day, depending on the
              assistance level, battery capacity and terrain.
            </p>
            <p>
              E-MTBs can also make it easier for a group with different fitness
              levels to share a suitable itinerary. Assistance does not make
              every rider equally fast, however, and technical confidence
              remains important. The route should allow the group to ride at a
              comfortable pace, regroup regularly and enjoy the surroundings
              instead of treating every stage as a race.
            </p>
            <figure className="article-inline-image">
              <img
                src="/images/atlas-mountains-cycling-tour-morocco.jpg"
                alt="Mountain biking through the Atlas Mountains in Morocco"
                loading="lazy"
              />
              <figcaption>
                Atlas Mountain routes combine changing landscapes with climbs
                and descents that reward careful itinerary planning.
              </figcaption>
            </figure>
          </section>

          <section id="where-to-ride">
            <h2>Where can you ride an E-MTB in Morocco?</h2>
            <h3>The Atlas Mountains</h3>
            <p>
              The Atlas region is a natural starting point for riders looking
              for mountain scenery, rural tracks and varied elevation. Routes
              may combine gradual climbs, rough mountain roads and narrower
              trails. The exact experience depends on the valley, altitude,
              trail access and the technical level selected for the group.
            </p>
            <p>
              Before choosing an itinerary, ask how much of the route is
              singletrack, rocky track or paved road. Find out whether any
              sections are steep, exposed or likely to require walking, and
              whether the planned stages offer suitable opportunities to
              recharge batteries.
            </p>
            <h3>Routes around Marrakech</h3>
            <p>
              Marrakech can provide a practical starting point for guided
              cycling experiences beyond the city. Depending on the itinerary,
              riders may travel towards mountain foothills, countryside or
              drier landscapes. Confirm transfer times and the actual riding
              location before comparing tours, because a trip described as
              starting in Marrakech may include a transfer before the ride
              begins.
            </p>
            <h3>Choose the landscape for your riding style</h3>
            <p>
              Some riders want flowing trails and long descents; others prefer
              scenic exploration with manageable climbs and time to visit
              villages. Describe your priorities to your organiser so the
              route can balance riding time, technical features, scenery and
              battery availability.
            </p>
            <div className="article-tip">
              <strong>Route tip</strong>
              <p>
                Ask for the route surface, elevation profile, expected riding
                time, technical difficulty and planned charging opportunities.
                These details are more useful than distance alone when
                choosing an E-MTB adventure.
              </p>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section id="choose-tour">
            <h2>Choose the right E-MTB tour for your experience</h2>
            <p>
              Start by considering what you enjoy on a mountain bike. Do you
              feel confident riding loose stones? Can you control your speed
              on steep descents? Are you comfortable riding for several hours,
              or would you prefer shorter stages with more time for stops?
              Your answers will help determine which itinerary is appropriate.
            </p>
            <h3>Scenic and introductory rides</h3>
            <p>
              Riders who are new to electric mountain biking may prefer wider
              tracks, moderate gradients and shorter stages. This allows time
              to become familiar with the motor response, the bike&apos;s
              handling and the process of managing assistance levels. A
              beginner-friendly tour should still be selected around the
              rider&apos;s actual cycling experience.
            </p>
            <h3>Technical trail and enduro-style riding</h3>
            <p>
              More experienced riders may want rocky singletrack, steeper
              descents and more demanding terrain. These routes require
              confident braking, line choice and balance. The additional
              weight of an E-MTB can change how it responds, so riders should
              understand the bike before tackling difficult sections.
            </p>
            <ul className="article-checklist">
              <li>Describe your recent mountain biking experience honestly.</li>
              <li>Ask about the steepest climbs and most technical descents.</li>
              <li>Confirm daily distance, elevation gain and riding time.</li>
              <li>Check whether the group can adjust the route if needed.</li>
              <li>Ask what guiding and mechanical support are provided.</li>
            </ul>
          </section>

          <section id="battery-range">
            <h2>Understand battery range and charging</h2>
            <p>
              Battery planning is one of the most important parts of an
              E-MTB trip. Range varies with battery capacity, assistance mode,
              rider weight, luggage, tyre pressure, temperature, surface and
              the amount of climbing. A route with repeated steep ascents and
              loose ground may use considerably more energy than a smoother
              ride of the same distance.
            </p>
            <p>
              Avoid planning a remote stage around a manufacturer's maximum
              advertised range. Instead, ask your organiser how the actual
              bike performs on comparable terrain and what reserve is
              recommended. If the itinerary includes long climbs or limited
              charging opportunities, the route and assistance strategy need
              to account for that.
            </p>
            <h3>Use assistance thoughtfully</h3>
            <p>
              Lower assistance settings can conserve energy on manageable
              terrain, while higher settings may be useful for demanding
              climbs. The right balance depends on your bike, the route and
              how you feel during the ride. Learn the controls before
              departure and avoid relying on one setting for every situation.
            </p>
            <h3>Confirm charging arrangements</h3>
            <p>
              Ask where charging can take place, whether chargers are
              compatible with the supplied bikes and how long charging is
              expected to take. For multi-day trips, confirm whether overnight
              charging is available and whether spare batteries are provided.
              Do not assume that every accommodation stop has the required
              facilities.
            </p>
            <div className="article-tip">
              <strong>Battery planning tip</strong>
              <p>
                Confirm the battery capacity, realistic range for your
                itinerary, charging schedule and backup plan before travelling.
                The exact equipment and route should determine the plan.
              </p>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section id="skills-fitness">
            <h2>Fitness, handling skills and riding confidence</h2>
            <p>
              Electric assistance reduces some of the effort needed to climb,
              but it does not remove the physical demands of a full day on the
              bike. Rough ground, repeated descents and changes in elevation
              can be tiring, and technical riding requires concentration.
              Build up your riding time before departure and include some
              climbing and descending practice where possible.
            </p>
            <p>
              Spend time becoming familiar with the bike before riding
              difficult terrain. Practise smooth starts, controlled braking,
              cornering and changing assistance modes on ground where you
              feel comfortable. An E-MTB can be heavier than a conventional
              mountain bike, so moving it through tight turns or lifting it
              over obstacles may require a different approach.
            </p>
            <p>
              If you are unsure about a section, stop and assess it. Walking a
              technical feature is a sensible decision, not a failure. Tell
              your guide about your comfort level so the pace and route can be
              adjusted appropriately.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section id="bike-equipment">
            <h2>Choose the right bike and equipment</h2>
            <p>
              A suitable E-MTB should fit the rider and the terrain. Frame
              size, braking performance, tyre choice, gearing and suspension
              all affect comfort and control. Before the trip, confirm the
              supplied bike model, size, battery setup, pedals and helmet
              arrangements. If you bring your own bike, ask about transport
              and compatibility with the tour's charging arrangements.
            </p>
            <h3>Essential riding equipment</h3>
            <ul className="article-checklist">
              <li>A properly fitted helmet and suitable cycling gloves.</li>
              <li>Comfortable riding clothing and protective eyewear.</li>
              <li>Riding shoes with suitable grip for the pedals provided.</li>
              <li>Water capacity, snacks and sun protection.</li>
              <li>Layers for changing temperatures and mountain weather.</li>
              <li>Any personal medication and essential travel documents.</li>
            </ul>
            <h3>Mechanical support and spare parts</h3>
            <p>
              Ask who carries repair tools and common spares, how punctures
              and mechanical problems are handled, and what happens if an
              electrical component fails. Battery and motor systems may
              require specialist support, so clarify the available backup
              arrangements before setting off on remote stages.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section id="seasons">
            <h2>Weather and the best time to ride</h2>
            <p>
              Riding conditions in Morocco vary by region, altitude and time
              of year. Mountain areas can be cool, windy or affected by rain
              and snow, while lower and exposed routes can become very hot.
              Weather also affects trail grip, visibility and how comfortable
              a long day on the bike will be.
            </p>
            <p>
              Spring and autumn are often considered for many mountain
              itineraries, but there is no single ideal season for every route.
              Check the forecast and local trail conditions close to your
              departure date. Build flexibility into the plan in case weather
              or access conditions make a particular stage unsuitable.
            </p>
            <p>
              Battery performance can also be affected by temperature. Follow
              the bike manufacturer's guidance for charging, storage and
              operating conditions, and ask your organiser how batteries will
              be handled during the trip.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section id="responsible-riding">
            <h2>Ride responsibly through Morocco's landscapes</h2>
            <p>
              Some routes pass through villages, agricultural land and paths
              shared with walkers and livestock. Slow down near homes, give
              people and animals plenty of space, and follow local guidance
              about trail access. Electric assistance can make it easy to
              approach others quietly, so remain alert and communicate
              considerately.
            </p>
            <p>
              Stay on appropriate paths, avoid widening trails around
              obstacles and carry out your rubbish. Ask before photographing
              people, respect private property and be mindful when stopping
              or regrouping. Local guides can help explain which routes are
              suitable and how to travel respectfully through communities.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section id="plan-trip">
            <h2>Plan your E-MTB adventure in Morocco</h2>
            <p>
              Begin with your preferred travel dates, group size, riding
              experience and the type of landscape you want to explore. Then
              compare route difficulty, daily elevation, accommodation,
              meals, transfers and mechanical support. For an electric bike
              itinerary, battery capacity and charging arrangements should be
              part of the planning discussion from the beginning.
            </p>
            <p>
              Before confirming a booking, ask for a clear explanation of what
              is included in the price. Check whether the E-MTB, charger,
              helmet, guide, luggage transfers, accommodation, meals and
              support vehicle are provided. Inclusions vary by tour, so get
              the details for your specific itinerary in writing.
            </p>
            <p>
              Explore our <Link href="/cycling-trips/e-mtb">E-MTB tours</Link>,
              discover <Link href="/destinations/atlas-mountains">Atlas Mountains destinations</Link>,
              or browse the full range of <Link href="/cycling-trips">cycling trips in Morocco</Link>.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section id="checklist">
            <h2>E-MTB trip checklist</h2>
            <ul className="article-checklist">
              <li>Confirm the bike model, size, battery capacity and charger.</li>
              <li>Understand realistic battery range for the planned terrain.</li>
              <li>Check charging access and backup arrangements for each stage.</li>
              <li>Review route surface, elevation, difficulty and daily riding time.</li>
              <li>Prepare your helmet, gloves, shoes, water and weather layers.</li>
              <li>Confirm guiding, mechanical support, transfers and inclusions.</li>
              <li>Check the forecast and any route access updates before departure.</li>
              <li>Respect local communities, shared paths and the environment.</li>
            </ul>
          </section>

          <section className="article-faq" id="faq" aria-labelledby="article-faq-title">
            <h2 id="article-faq-title">Frequently asked questions</h2>
            {faqs.map((faq) => (
